  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 135 PM Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Temperatures will trend to well above normal this \nweek, hottest Thu and Fri. \n\nSurface high centered near the Mid Atlantic coast will continue an \neastward drift over the next few days, while continuing to extend SW \nacross central NC. As deep 850-500 mb W-to-E ridging spans just to \nour S through Wed, cutting off the Gulf/Atlantic, the resulting \nabundant sunshine will allow temps to heat up, and despite near-\nnormal thicknesses Wed, the insolation will boost highs to near \nnormal values. Low level thicknesses will climb further and peak \nThu/Fri, with readings 15-20 m above normal, supporting highs around \n2 categories above normal, mainly in the upper 80s to mid 90s. Heat \nindex values will top out in the 90s Thu and 90s to around 102 Fri, \nas surface dewpoints creep upward ahead of the approaching cold \nfront. This front will bring accompanying clouds and increasing rain \nchances Fri into the weekend which should curb the heat a bit, \nalthough the glancing blow of the weakening shortwave trough passing \nby to our N will be quickly followed by another ridge building over \nthe Carolinas and Southeast states early next week, which will \nlikely mean a return to hot temperatures by Mon/Tue.  \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Generally dry weather through Thu night will yield \nto increased convection chances Fri through Sun, highest on Sat. \n\nSurface high pressure will continue to extend SW across central NC \nas it drifts further off the Mid Atlantic coast through Wed night. \nThis will keep us within deeply dry and stable air, with low PWs and \nno pops. The 925-850 mb ridging stretching from OK/TX east to off \nthe Southeast coast will limit significant Gulf/Atlantic moisture \ninflux and keep most of the area free of rain chances through Thu, \nalthough a brief surge of 1.5\"+ PW into SE NC Thu may provide \nsufficient moisture for a few late-day sea-breeze storms. The mid \nlevel ridge then breaks down by Thu as a broad and positively-tilted \nnorthern stream trough sweeps through the Upper Midwest and Great \nLakes, with its accompanying surface cold front approaching our area \nfrom the NW late Thu. This will resulting in increasing moisture \nflux areawide and a rebound to near or above normal PW by Fri. With \nthe increasing mid level flow helping to boost deep layer bulk shear \nand rising low level moisture fostering increased daytime CAPE, the \nconfidence in the chance for afternoon and evening showers and \nstorms for Fri and Sat is also increasing. With the dip in the \nwesterlies reaching down closer to our latitude and resulting in \npassing of multiple weak perturbations over NC, these higher \nconvection chances may linger into Sun, particularly over our S and \nE, as the surface front slow then stalls over our region. Models \nproject that this troughing will weaken considerably and dissipate \nby Sun night/Mon as strong mid level ridging builds once again from \nthe Mid South into the Carolinas, so rain chances should drop back \nto generally isolated by Mon/Tue.",
